1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,384
What happens when the payment goes through, but shipping fails?

2
00:00:04,334 --> 00:00:08,553
Your customer is charged, the stock is locked, and nothing ships.

3
00:00:08,553 --> 00:00:11,558
Today we fix that with the Saga pattern.

4
00:00:21,812 --> 00:00:24,118
Here is the product problem.

5
00:00:24,068 --> 00:00:27,133
To book an order we call three services:

6
00:00:27,133 --> 00:00:30,198
reserve inventory, charge the payment, and create the shipment.

7
00:00:30,198 --> 00:00:32,848
Each one has its own database, so there

8
00:00:32,848 --> 00:00:35,498
is no single transaction that covers all three.

9
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:40,016
The inputs are simple: an order id, a SKU,

10
00:00:40,016 --> 00:00:42,972
a quantity, the amount in cents, and an address.

11
00:00:42,922 --> 00:00:46,407
On success, the order is committed and shipped.

12
00:00:46,407 --> 00:00:51,119
On failure, we must refund the money and release the stock, in reverse order.

13
00:00:54,868 --> 00:00:58,268
This is the naive version most of us write first.

14
00:00:58,218 --> 00:01:03,121
Reserve, then charge, then ship, with an early return on every failure.

15
00:01:03,121 --> 00:01:05,165
Look at the comments.

16
00:01:05,165 --> 00:01:08,698
If the charge fails, inventory stays reserved.

17
00:01:08,698 --> 00:01:12,280
If shipping fails, the customer has already paid.

18
00:01:13,836 --> 00:01:16,238
So the naive flow leaks.

19
00:01:16,188 --> 00:01:20,707
There is no compensation registry, no way to inject a failure in a test,

20
00:01:20,707 --> 00:01:25,226
and no idempotency key, which means a retry can double charge the customer in production.

21
00:01:28,980 --> 00:01:30,853
Enter the Saga.

22
00:01:30,803 --> 00:01:33,814
An orchestrator calls each step in order,

23
00:01:33,814 --> 00:01:36,825
and every successful step registers a compensating action.

24
00:01:36,825 --> 00:01:39,974
Inventory reserve is undone by release.

25
00:01:39,974 --> 00:01:42,510
Charge is undone by refund.

26
00:01:42,510 --> 00:01:45,653
There is also a choreography twin, where services react

27
00:01:45,653 --> 00:01:48,796
to events on a bus instead of a central coordinator.

28
00:01:52,548 --> 00:01:55,142
Here is the orchestrated saga.

29
00:01:55,092 --> 00:01:57,965
Three small interfaces, inventory, payments and shipping,

30
00:01:57,965 --> 00:02:00,837
each with a do and an undo method.

31
00:02:00,837 --> 00:02:04,419
A step result record tells us whether a call succeeded.

32
00:02:05,972 --> 00:02:10,622
Inside book, every step runs once, keyed by order id and step name.

33
00:02:10,572 --> 00:02:13,144
That is our idempotency guard.

34
00:02:13,144 --> 00:02:17,404
After a step succeeds, we add its compensation to the front of

35
00:02:17,404 --> 00:02:21,665
the list, so when something throws, the compensations replay in reverse order automatically.

36
00:02:23,212 --> 00:02:26,816
And this is real output from JDK 25.

37
00:02:26,766 --> 00:02:30,925
In the happy path we see reserve, charge, ship, committed.

38
00:02:30,925 --> 00:02:34,320
When the carrier is down, the saga aborts at ship,

39
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:37,715
refunds the payment, releases the stock, and ends in rolled back.

40
00:02:37,715 --> 00:02:40,852
No leaked money, no locked inventory.

41
00:02:44,596 --> 00:02:46,565
Now the trade-offs.

42
00:02:46,515 --> 00:02:50,614
Use a saga when a business transaction really spans services.

43
00:02:50,614 --> 00:02:55,301
Do not reach for it when one database transaction or a simple function would do.

44
00:02:55,301 --> 00:02:58,967
And remember, compensations are not a magic undo.

45
00:02:58,967 --> 00:03:03,462
They are new business actions, so they must be idempotent and well tested.

46
00:03:07,228 --> 00:03:08,945
Quick recap.

47
00:03:08,895 --> 00:03:13,198
Start from the problem, feel the naive pain, then name the pattern.

48
00:03:13,198 --> 00:03:16,395
Register a compensation for every step, run them in

49
00:03:16,395 --> 00:03:19,592
reverse on failure, and inject failures in your tests.

50
00:03:19,592 --> 00:03:23,102
All the code and slides are on GitHub, linked below.

51
00:03:23,102 --> 00:03:26,455
I am Waqar, and I will see you in the next one.

